Practical Implementation of Build Tool – Maven for Java Project ☕⚙️
As part of my DevSecOps hands-on learning, I practically implemented Apache Maven to build, test, and package a Java application. Understanding build tools at a practical level is essential for CI/CD automation and secure software delivery.

Here’s the complete step-by-step implementation I followed:
🔹 Step 1: Install Java (JDK)
Maven requires Java to be installed first.
✔ Install Java (recommended: JDK 17 or above)
✔ Set JAVA_HOME environment variable
✔ Verify installation using:
java -version
javac -version
This confirms that both Java runtime and compiler are properly configured.

🔹 Step 2: Install Apache Maven
✔ Download and install Apache Maven
✔ Configure MAVEN_HOME
✔ Add Maven bin directory to system PATH
✔ Verify installation using:
mvn -version
If configured correctly, it displays Maven version along with Java version being used.

🔹 Step 3: Clone the GitHub Repository
git clone https://lnkd.in/gPHkDEjx
cd <repo-name>
Inside the project directory, the pom.xml file acts as the heart of Maven. It defines:
• Project metadata
• Dependencies
• Plugins
• Build lifecycle

🔹 Step 4: Run Test Cases Using Maven
mvn test
This step:
• Downloads required dependencies
• Compiles source code
• Executes test cases
• Generates test reports
Ensures code reliability before packaging.

🔹 Step 5: Create the JAR File
mvn package
Maven executes lifecycle phases:
Validate → Compile → Test → Package
After successful execution, the .jar file will be available inside the target/ directory.

🔹 Step 6: Run the Java Application
java -jar target/<filename>.jar
If everything is configured correctly, the application will start successfully.

🔐 Why Maven Matters in DevSecOps
✔ Automated dependency management
✔ Standardized build lifecycle
✔ Easy CI/CD integration (Jenkins, GitHub Actions, GitLab CI)
✔ Security plugin support (OWASP Dependency Check, Snyk)
✔ Reproducible builds

💡 Key Learning:
Practical implementation of Maven strengthens CI/CD automation skills and helps build secure, reliable Java applications in DevSecOps environments.
